' SEMVER CONTRACT: read the actual image/native producer version before selecting config or history stores.
' Partition immutable defaults, persistent configuration, history and active caches by harness + compatibility major.
' Implemented paths: opencode/v2/config and opencode/v2/share; other majors and unversioned paths remain separate.
' An empty V2 share copies one existing legacy opencode.db once, then prepares that copy. The legacy files stay unchanged.
' A V1 launch must never open a V2-mutated database.
' Unknown/unversioned state requires explicit inspection and adoption through maintenance.
' Session row versions are historical content; they do not identify the last database producer.
' The native two-run/resume regression includes an adopted V1 session version (1.18.21).
' Prerelease 0.x builds require an explicit compatibility epoch; do not infer stable V2 from an executable name.
' Same-major compatibility still requires the expected schema/migration fingerprint.
' An unprepared schema transition belongs in maintenance even when the native package's major did not change.
'
' IMPLEMENTED AUTHORITY: a non-dumpable engine-local keeper owns prepared state in memory.
' The host launches it with isolated Python and records its kernel PID/start-time outside writable guest mounts.
' Every managed native checkout verifies that identity through SO_PEERCRED and the kernel process start time.
' Mutable readiness JSON and a replacement same-UID socket cannot authorize a checkout.
' State records include exact producer version, schema digest, engine, file identities and prepared/active/pending state.
' Production keepers load baked helper/version/native paths rather than agent-provided helper overrides.
' Keep receipt format, producer semver and schema compatibility as separate fields.
' The host/runtime owner issues descriptors outside agent-writable shared state.
' An agent-written receipt or mtime comparison cannot authorize an unsanitized fast path.
'
' SELECTED SEED PHASE: verify a trusted prepared-generation descriptor plus native-major/schema compatibility.
' Do not run full backup, VACUUM, SQL dump hashing or implicit legacy import during seeding.
' An unprepared engine prepares the mounted V2 database in this launch and reports the three steps.
' Pending recovery is the host's: an ordinary launch recovers or asks (SBX AUTO-STOP and NO MAINTENANCE COMMANDS below).
' Empty preparation is not a hidden fallback over an existing V2 database.
' Seed configuration only after selecting the matching-major namespace.
'
' SELECTED LAUNCH PHASE: reuse the credential-free prepared cache on its owning runtime engine.
' Check compatibility, generation state and exclusive lifecycle ownership, then enter the native client.
' Remove the five pre-native full snapshots; retain full private-state and credential isolation guarantees.
' Changing cache retention does not authorize retaining an unsanitized native runtime directory.
'
' REDESIGN: retain one prepared native history cache per major and owning runtime engine.
' Keep SQLite active state on the engine's local filesystem, not a cross-VM shared WAL database.
' Keep the native data prefix stable across runs so ordinary startup does not rewrite stored JSON paths.
' Acquire lifecycle ownership once and reuse compatible prepared state instead of copying all history per run.
' Preserve descendant lifetime ownership and explicit busy-run rejection.
' Keep the existing three post-exit snapshot/publication calls; do not optimize that phase in this redesign.
' Reuse an already-verified post-exit result for prepared-cache retention without adding another bulk snapshot.
' Preserve the previous good generation until the new generation publishes atomically.
' Reconcile host-visible history and engine-local state during maintenance; never merge on implicit startup. Re-verifying a lost keeper's cache (SBX AUTO-STOP) is recovery, not a merge.
' Treat an interrupted generation whose private cache still exists as pending recovery without deleting its record.
' A removed engine prepares the mounted database when this cache and the journal's recorded cache are both gone, the database matches, and the producer shares the running stable major.
' Device, inode and timestamp drift are not a baseline. The current durable files are imported, then that snapshot is pinned. A later edit does not become the new baseline.
' Unpublished sessions from the removed engine are gone with it. Another major is pending private state, which the launch recovers or asks about. A private cache that still exists is never overwritten; SBX AUTO-STOP below says when a launch recovers it.
' Prepare, recover and a busy history check report the runtime failure text. Exit 75 is not relabeled as an agent exit.
' A cold engine with a mounted V2 database is prepared before the agent starts. The native client does not start on an unprepared cache.
' sbx creates/bootstrap-checks the keeper before managed launch and retains successful OpenCode engines.
' Ordinary V2 launches omit legacy history mounts.
' Changed launch settings retire a prepared or empty idle engine, not an active/unpublished one.
' Fresh empty state is prepared without importing old/unversioned history; an existing legacy database is copied and prepared.
' proveo adds no maintenance commands; native flags pass through unchanged (NO MAINTENANCE COMMANDS below).
' An unrecoverable engine asks before sbx rm --force, for every harness. Yes deletes the session.
' The next proveo run creates a new sandbox with the same name. That session does not persist.
' No keeps the sandbox, and the next proveo run reattaches to it.
' A kept sandbox whose image, kit, mounts, clone or env differ asks the same way.
' The steps are carry work home, sbx rm --force, then create that same name.
' A headed yes does not remove an OpenCode engine with active, unpublished, or unattested state, except from the SBX AUTO-STOP recovery prompts, which name the loss first.
' A direct checkout keeps host edits. A clone loses uncommitted edits unless they were fetched.
' A screen that fails to open counts as no.
' A V2 share with a database and an unprepared engine prepares that database.
'
' DOCKER DOCS, read 2026-10-14 — why history is prepared by the host, between create and run:
'   https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/architecture/ : everything inside a sandbox "persists until you remove it",
'     "agent state and history" included; "sbx rm" deletes "all of its contents".
'   https://github.com/docker/sbx-releases/releases v0.42.0: a new sandbox reusing a deleted name "no longer inherits
'     its files, Docker images, or agent session history". So the prepared cache lives in the retained engine.
'   https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/customize/kits-v2/ : startup commands "don't gate the agent's entrypoint";
'     "Use setup.files for any value that needs to land on disk before the agent runs". setup.files writes text
'     content, and a kit "can't read arbitrary host environment variables or files"; v2 volumes take no host source.
'   https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/agents/opencode/ documents no data or history path and no import.
' No kit surface can carry a SQLite history store in. The V2 share reaches the engine through the proveo home,
' which proveo mounts as a workspace; the host prepares it through `sbx exec`, then re-runs proveo-seed with
' PROVEO_OPENCODE_CACHE_OWNER and reattaches the agent through the kit. `--shell` skips that sequence, so an
' opencode --shell sandbox stays refused and unseeded; e2e/seed_ready_test.go runs opencode through its kit.
'
' SBX AUTO-STOP LOSES THE KEEPER, 2026-10-14 — research and the Executor's decision:
'   "By default, a local sandbox stops automatically shortly after its last session disconnects"
'     https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/customize/author/ ; 30s grace (docker/sbx-releases#452);
'     `sbx create` sandboxes since v0.43.0. "The rest of the VM filesystem persists across restarts"
'     (https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/security/isolation/); processes and memory do not.
' So the keeper, whose attestation lives only in its memory, dies ~30s after every run. The
' engine-local cache under /tmp survives. The next run's --bootstrap-cache starts a fresh keeper
' (state unprepared, no cache signature); --check-prepared exits 78 and --prepare refuses:
' "unattested retained state requires explicit recovery, not replacement". Measured on a Linux
' host 2026-10-14 (proveo-opencode-11c4aed0, reattach after an earlier run). OpenCode plays no part
' (https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/cli/ documents no attestation); the in-memory keeper design does.
' DECISION (Executor, "Auto after clean stop"): on that refusal, an ordinary launch reads the
' durable journal (opencode/v2/share/.proveo-opencode-runtime.lock.journal) as untrusted input:
' a regular file under 64 KiB, its phase one lowercase word. The journal is per STORE, shared by
' every engine on one proveo home, so its engine field (the sandbox name, SANDBOX_VM_ID) decides.
'   this engine, prepared | published -> --recover at once, one notice line, no prompt.
'   this engine, other phase, or no journal -> headed: choiceui.ChooseRetainedRecovery
'     (y recover, r reset = sbx rm --force then this launch prepares again, n/esc stop: the next
'     run asks again, or the reset command); headless (PROVEO_WIZARD=off, schedule, no TTY) -> recover.
'   another engine, prepared | published -> this copy is stale and the runtime would reject it
'     ("belongs to another engine"): headed reset prompt, headless error naming sbx rm --force.
'   another engine, any other phase -> that engine may be live: error, no recover, no reset.
'   recover fails -> busy store or timeout: error, never a reset (the keeper may still publish);
'     otherwise the runtime text, then headed: the reset prompt; headless: the sbx rm command.
' A reset inside a launch keeps the launch receipt: this launch recreates the engine with the
' same settings, and a missing receipt made the next run refuse the engine as unrecorded.
' Headless never deletes. Recovery re-verifies and republishes and is never
' a fast-path receipt. [Inference] A large history recovers slower than the 5s budget.
' Container evidence, 2026-10-15: `docker restart` (processes die, /tmp stays) reproduced the
' Linux host's sequence exactly, check 78 then the prepare refusal, and --recover re-attested
' the cache (check 0; journal engine = SANDBOX_VM_ID, phase prepared).
' [Unverified] durable_stamp hashes st_dev, st_ino and ctime of the mounted share; if the
' guest's mount device number changes across a VM restart, every recovery fails with
' "published history changed" and this path falls to the reset. The e2e below measures it.
' Follow-ups: keep the engine up between runs (`sbx run -d` / long-running@1, sbx >= v0.47.0),
' or a host-held cache digest taken after native shutdown and a WAL checkpoint (OpenCode writes
' the DB on every open).
' e2e/opencode_cache_sbx_test.go keeps the operator's HOME, where sbx keeps its login: with a
' temporary HOME, `sbx template load` failed "Not authenticated to Docker" (2026-10-15). Only
' PROVEO_HOME is temporary; the engine is named by the run's "retained engine" line. Cleanup
' removes every proveo-opencode-<hash> engine any run printed, failed runs included,
' with its owner record and receipt from the user cache dir.
' e2e/opencode_cache_sbx_test.go stops the engine with `sbx stop` between two reattaches and
' requires the session to survive and the run to report re-verifying its retained history.
' READ-ONLY WAL SOURCE, 2026-10-15 — why --proveo-prepare --source always failed (the command is gone;
' readonly_connect stays for any read-only WAL store):
' The source is mounted read-only by design, and every OpenCode 2.x database is in WAL mode; a clean
' exit removes -wal and -shm. database_schema (and the credential helper's database_snapshot and
' inventory) opened it with `?mode=ro`, and SQLite cannot open a WAL database in a directory where it
' cannot create the -shm file: "sqlite3.OperationalError: unable to open database file"
' ([Unverified] https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html, "Read-Only Databases"). The keeper turned that into
' "OpenCode cache operation failed; explicit recovery required", so e2e/opencode_cache_sbx_test.go
' failed with no cause; the traceback came from a container repro with a patched keeper.
' readonly_connect (packages/lib/opencode-credentials.py) now opens such a file with `immutable=1`
' when its directory is not writable and no -shm exists; the source lease and the before/after
' source signature already reject a source that changes during import. A non-empty -wal with no
' -shm in a read-only directory is refused by name: its frames cannot be read without a -shm.
' The keeper now passes SnapshotError text to the client, as it does CacheError: both are proveo's
' own words. Any other exception still reads as the generic message, and its traceback is kept
' nowhere; a private keeper log would have to keep tracebacks away from the agent's uid.
' internal/imagetest/opencode_test.go runs both paths in the image: a 0555 WAL source prepares,
' then a killed keeper's cache is refused by check and prepare and re-attested by --recover.
' HEADLESS MAINTENANCE, 2026-10-15 — SUPERSEDED by NO MAINTENANCE COMMANDS, kept as history:
' After --proveo-prepare --source, the run started the agent with no native arguments, which is the
' OpenCode TUI. Measured in proveo/opencode:local: the native TUI with no TTY and stdin at EOF never
' exits (killed at 40s). The e2e captures output without a TTY, and sbx failed with
' "error: inspect exec: context deadline exceeded". [Inference] sbx ends the attach at stdin EOF and
' times out waiting for an exec that is still running; no sbx doc or issue names that message.
' DECISION (Executor, "Stop after prepare"): --proveo-prepare or --proveo-recover with no native
' arguments and no terminal (stdin and stdout not both TTYs) finishes the cache work, prints
' "no terminal — the agent was not started" and that the next run starts the agent, then exits 0
' before the seed and sbx run. A terminal, or any native command after the flags, starts the agent
' as before. An ordinary launch without a terminal is unchanged. The engine keeps a prepared journal,
' so a later run that finds the keeper gone recovers it at once (SBX AUTO-STOP above).
' The run log records "no agent was launched", not "the agent exited 0", and the home access stays
' with the retained engine, as after an agent run.
' The owner record holds the keeper's pid:starttime, so it changes whenever sbx restarts the engine;
' the e2e therefore proves "not replaced" by no later run printing "Created sandbox <engine>" (which
' sbx create printed in the prepare run, 2026-10-15) or proveo's "creates a new sandbox named".
' Its cleanup also removes the engine names sbx.SandboxName derives from the workspace, so a run
' killed before it printed a name leaks nothing.
' TERMINAL LEGS, 2026-10-16 — why the first reattach printed no session (the pipe prepare leg is gone
' with NO MAINTENANCE COMMANDS; every leg now runs on the pty):
' With the prepare leg passing, `-- api POST /api/session` on a pipe, with stdin at EOF (/dev/null),
' exited 0 and printed only sbx's "Starting opencode agent in sandbox" and "Workspace:" lines: no
' image banner, no JSON. proveo copies stdout through when there is no TTY (agentio.Stdio), and the
' runtime wrapper does not redirect it. [Inference] `sbx run` with no TTY and stdin at EOF does not
' return the agent's output; the run did not separate the two conditions, its reference page is
' empty, and no issue names this. A run is interactive by construction
' (_spec/internal/sbx/sandbox-backend.puml), and every other sbx e2e drives proveo through tmux or
' a pty.
' DECISION (Executor, "PTY legs"): the prepare leg stays on a pipe, because it tests HEADLESS
' MAINTENANCE. The api POST, api GET and post-stop api GET legs run proveo on a 200x40 pty
' (creack/pty). Accepted gap, chosen over a proveo warning: under sbx, a scripted native command
' with no terminal gets no agent output and still exits 0.
' Each leg requires proveo's "re-attaching to <engine>," and no "Created sandbox <engine>" or
' "creates a new sandbox named <engine>"; the GET legs require the session id the POST returned.
' Pty output is read as terminalText: control sequences removed, each carriage return a line
' break. The seed wait draws "\r\x1b[2Kproveo: Waiting for seed" with no newline, so on a pty the
' agent's JSON shares that line; plain() would also insert a space where a sequence was. NO_COLOR=1
' keeps proveo from wrapping. Measured in proveo/opencode:local: native `api` prints one JSON line
' on a TTY and on a pipe.
' Each new "[A]pprove all" gets "A" and Enter, matched with whitespace removed. [Speculation] sbx
' may style or cursor-space that prompt. Approval saves a per-service binding in the operator's
' ~/.config/sbx/credentials.yaml, which outlives the test. Any prompt brokenPrompt names ends the leg
' at once rather than at its 3-minute deadline.
' creack/pty opens /dev/ptmx blocking on darwin, so closing it does not end a read; the reads drain
' in a goroutine, and the leg returns at most 10s after proveo exits.
' NO MAINTENANCE COMMANDS, 2026-10-16 — DECISION (Executor, "Drop commands + self-prepare"):
' proveo no longer accepts --proveo-prepare [--source DIR|--empty] or --proveo-recover. They were not the
' V1->V2 path: an ordinary launch copies a legacy opencode.db into opencode/v2/share and prepares the copy,
' and --source refused V1 stores ("V1/unknown stores require separate explicit migration"). An ordinary
' launch already recovered retained history (SBX AUTO-STOP above). HEADLESS MAINTENANCE, the temporary
' -prepare engine and ErrAgentNotStarted go with them.
' To bring an external V2 store, copy its opencode.db into <proveo home>/opencode/v2/share before the
' first launch; the launch prepares that mounted V2 database.
' Every refusal that named --proveo-recover now reaches recoverRetainedOpenCode:
'   prepare refuses with the retained refusal, or with "pending private state" (an interrupted
'   publication, or another major's journal) -> recoverRetainedOpenCode;
'   --check-prepared exits 75 because the keeper is pending recovery, not busy -> prepare -> the same.
'   A busy store (75, "owns") still stops the launch with the runtime text.
' Runtime messages name `proveo run opencode` instead of the removed commands.
' Measured 2026-10-16 on a Docker-only Linux host: the prepared-history gate, not the commands, refused
' launches that bypass the host. The in-image presentation fixture failed "unprepared in this engine".
' The seed refusal text was the one the operator's e2e-full reported for opencode; ladder rung 3 runs
' `sbx run --kit` with proveo's kit and no host (e2e/ladder_test.go). [Inference] that rung is its source.
' UNMANAGED SELF-PREPARE. A managed launch carries --proveo-cache-owner on the agent command; a raw
' kit launch does not, so the owner flag is the discriminator. The startup seed cannot decide: a managed
' launch runs the same kit seed before the host's --bootstrap-cache, and bootstrap refuses a live keeper
' with no host record ("a cache controller already exists without a trusted host owner record").
' proveo-await-seed, when the seed refused with "OpenCode history is not prepared in this engine" and
' the runtime command has no owner flag before `--`, runs `proveo-opencode-runtime --self-prepare`.
' --self-prepare refuses an owner flag, an existing keeper socket, and a legacy opencode.db beside an
' absent V2 database (a managed `proveo run opencode` adopts that one). Otherwise it starts a keeper and
' prepares the V2 database when present, else an empty one, and prints the owner the keeper's socket
' credentials name. await-seed then starts the seed with PROVEO_OPENCODE_CACHE_OWNER in the background
' (as the host's seedOpenCode does), waits for the instructions marker, and execs the runtime with
' --proveo-cache-owner. Checkout still requires an owner; the managed path is unchanged.
' [Inference] An unmanaged keeper has no host-recorded identity, so nothing outside the guest can tell a
' later replacement from it; only that one launch trusts it. The host's own bootstrap still refuses it.
' e2e/opencode_cache_sbx_test.go seeds the V2 database by copying it into the share, not with --source.
' internal/imagetest/opencode_test.go runs the unmanaged path in the image with no host.
' A V2 share with no database copies one legacy opencode.db into that share and prepares the copy. Native V2 migration runs on the copy.
' The legacy files stay in place. An older directory with no database still asks before an empty prepare.
' The checkpoint step is reported after prepare succeeds.
' No on that empty question leaves the sandbox up and asks again.
' After a confirmed delete, a failed recreate says the previous sandbox was removed.
' The durable copy of a legacy database is prepared so the native server can migrate it.
' Preparation initializes compatible schemas with the pinned native release,
' then runs the existing sanitized publication sequence outside ordinary startup.
' Diagnostics and commit-subject helpers use disposable empty private state rather than cloning all history.
' Docker-only disposable engines do not supply a retained keeper; the managed fast-start backend is sbx.
' Docker compatibility explicitly selects disposable sanitized preparation; it does not claim the sbx cache latency guarantee.
' The runtime's internal `--prepare --source` control has no proveo entry point since NO MAINTENANCE COMMANDS.
' The imported source never remains mounted in the retained agent engine, and its launch receipt stays stable on reattach.
' Stopped or unattested engines are not automatically retired when configuration changes.
' Published-state fingerprints cover the whole artifact/state tree except lifecycle locks and private recovery directories.
' Checkout acquires exclusive lifecycle ownership before comparing that fingerprint.
' Recovery requires a matching producer/engine/database/root and a persisted durable baseline.
' Post-exit writes transaction phase and publication baseline before rotating cache generations.
' Journal updates use fsynced temporary files and atomic replacement without replacing the lifecycle-lock inode.
' Idle prepared caches retain a recovery baseline but restarted authorities never treat disk state as a fast-path receipt.
' Durable publication persists per-file rename intents and content digests before replacement.
' File, directory-creation and deletion intents append to an fsynced linear log rather than rewriting a growing manifest.
' Every intent append syncs the log then its parent directory before authorizing a durable mutation.
' Interrupted publication accepts only unchanged baseline files or files matching its recorded intents.
' Native preferences restore from the retained full sanitized state image after partial cache rotation.
' Lifecycle journals are control-plane files excluded from every history snapshot and artifact link.
' Disposable Docker mode refuses a pending engine-owned cache journal before touching history.
' Launch receipts include the stored sbx agent type; shell/native changes cannot silently reuse the wrong agent.
' Dead implicit-history merge/digest helpers and their unused host forwarding are removed.
' Auth-file sanitization remains an import boundary, not a supported plaintext credential persistence mechanism.
' Recovery reconciles a retained previous-data directory and cleans only private publication transaction directories.
'
' CREDENTIAL GATE: an idle reusable cache and published generation must remain credential-free.
' Sanitize after every native writer and descendant stops, before marking that cache prepared again.
' Raw OAuth/API tokens must not gain persistence through cache retention.
' The host-owned OAuth proposal remains planned and requires its own pinned-connector verification.
' Do not rely on that unfinished proposal to claim a clean or fast active store today.
' Reject a design that only bypasses token removal, drops history or reads mutable data under a forged clean receipt.
'
' VERIFICATION GATES:
' - actual V1/V2 fixtures select disjoint stores; wrong-major paths remain unopened and byte-identical;
' - matching-major adoption verifies schema, every retained session transcript and artifact path;
' - prepared launches call neither legacy migration nor full backup/VACUUM/iterdump/rebase;
' - native session list and resume work on representative 879MB+ history within the <=5s startup budget;
' - cold/mismatch/busy/damaged cases resolve within the budget with maintenance/recovery guidance;
' - tokens disappear from idle cache, generation files and journals after login, refresh and interruption;
' - signal, descendant, cross-engine, disk-full and generation-publication failure fixtures retain recoverable state;
' - missing cache or another engine's record never causes reset, overwrite or cross-major fallback.
' - post-exit sanitization, recovery publication and durable publication preserve their existing behavior;
' - only a verified credential-free post-exit result can become the next prepared cache.
' Performance acceptance must measure successful native readiness, not just a five-second timeout or fake executable.
' Persisted installed-V2 fixture: a prepared 900MiB payload database produced native session-list output in 0.595s.
' The full test took 48.047s including explicit preparation and unchanged post-exit publication.
' After the review repairs, all eight installed-native tests passed; prepared 900MiB readiness measured 0.472s.
' Full Go verification reproduces three Docker run-golden failures and the credential-posture failure on unchanged HEAD.
' Docker plan goldens now assert the explicit disposable history mode and container ownership identifiers.
' Unit fixtures cover owner identity mismatch, forged ready files, cache mutation, wrong-major rejection and private recovery.
' Actual cross-VM locking, retained-sbx startup and rebuilt-image execution require host-side acceptance.
' FK (manual syllables): VERSION 10.8 (29w/51s); ACTIVE 11.0 (31w/54s); MAINTAIN 11.8 (28w/52s).
'
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
title OpenCode prepared history — implemented; live sbx acceptance pending
left to right direction

actor "Executor\nlaunch or explicit maintenance" as OP
component "Native/image version resolver\nbaked stable 2.x + native version check" as VERSION <<app>>
database "Host cache owner record\nkernel PID/start-time outside guest mounts" as CATALOG <<db>>
component "opencode_cache.py\nprepared gate + runtime keeper" as OWNER <<host>>
database "Engine-local active history\nstable prefix; prepared generation" as ACTIVE <<db>>
component "OpenCode native client\nmatching major only" as NATIVE <<app>>
component "Maintenance / post-exit publisher\nhost prepare + recover; three snapshots" as MAINTAIN <<app>>
database "Versioned credential-free checkpoints\nprevious good generation retained" as DURABLE <<db>>
database "V1 or unknown history\nuntouched by V2 launch" as OLD <<db>>
component "Preparation required\nbounded, explicit diagnostic" as REQUIRED <<error>>

OP --> VERSION : launch
VERSION --> CATALOG : select matching major + schema
CATALOG --> OWNER : prepared engine/generation
OWNER --> ACTIVE : acquire compatible lifecycle lease
ACTIVE --> NATIVE : reuse; no full-history startup copy
OWNER --> MAINTAIN : unprepared V2 database, this launch
OWNER --> REQUIRED : busy / pending recovery / declined empty prepare
OP --> MAINTAIN : explicit import / recover
OLD --> MAINTAIN : only approved import or adoption
NATIVE --> MAINTAIN : after all writers stop
MAINTAIN --> ACTIVE : prepared credential-free cache
MAINTAIN --> DURABLE : one verified atomic generation
MAINTAIN --> CATALOG : issue prepared descriptor

note bottom of VERSION
  Check the native major before opening history
  so startup cannot import incompatible data.
  Keep the schema stamp apart from receipt format
  and old session versions when choosing a store.
end note

note bottom of ACTIVE
  Reuse prepared history on the owning engine
  without copying all retained session payloads.
  Keep authentication tokens out of saved state
  before an idle cache or checkpoint is declared
  ready for reuse.
end note

note bottom of MAINTAIN
  Run bulk preparation outside normal startup
  so the spinner does not conceal the slow work.
  Keep source history and the previous good
  checkpoint until verification allows
  atomic publication.
end note
@enduml
